This is Eddie's BKPT Debug screen-recording rehearsal for the STM32 Zephyr
ViewAlyzer demo in this directory. Read these rules when asked to run the demo.

Invocation: "Follow .rules and run Act 1." "Run the demo" or "Run the rehearsal"
also means Act 1 only. Continue with "Continue Act 2", then "Continue Act 3".
Keep all three acts and their explicit handoffs. Do not start the next act until
Eddie asks. This firmware demonstrates intentional mutex contention, not the
Renode delayed-fault fixture. Do not invent fault controls or induce a fault.

Connection and authority
- Use the bkpt_debug tool from the bkpt-debug-vscode MCP server in this VS Code
  window. Read the installed BKPT Debug skill if available; this file supplies
  the rehearsal flow. If the server is unavailable, tell Eddie to click
  "Install MCP for Claude Code..." in the BKPT Debug sidebar, then start a new
  Claude conversation. Do not launch another Toolkit, GDB or probe owner.
- Use normal file tools to read source. All debugger operations, including
  watches, breakpoints and target inspection, go through bkpt_debug. Do not
  automate Eddie's mouse, keyboard, focus or windows.
- Read status and catalog, then describe each needed operation once per host
  identity. Use the current schemas, not remembered arguments. request_control
  requires the extension's human approval; the MCP adapter retains the grant.
  Reuse an existing target session. connected reports the Toolkit engine
  connection, NOT whether a board is attached. catalog can make connected:true
  while sessionId is still empty and targets is still []. This is normal.
- Decide whether to attach from sessionId and targets, not connected. With an
  empty sessionId, empty targets, no pendingOperations, recorded:false and
  cleanupUnconfirmed:false, obtain control and call action:"attach". attach
  needs NO coreId or target identity: it creates the first target session in
  this IDE's existing engine and returns its identity. It uses the IDE's probe,
  ELF and recording preferences; it does not take Zed's record:true argument.
  The extension rejects a second attach if a session already exists. If a
  nonempty sessionId has targets, reuse them. If operations are pending, allow
  a bounded wait and refresh status. If sessionId is nonempty but targets stay
  empty, or cleanup is unconfirmed, report that inconsistency instead of
  attaching. Recorded playback is not a live target; ask Eddie to close it.
- execute uses args containing operation, args, coreId and the complete target
  identity. Use the identity returned for the current stop/state. Refresh status
  after a state change if its result did not provide a fresh identity. Use
  request-local frame/contextId for saved task inspection; return to the live
  core before run control. Saved contexts expire when execution resumes.
- Use the probe, core and ELF configured in this project. The local filming
  setup is a NUCLEO-G474RE with ST-Link; confirm current configuration rather
  than choosing the first available probe. The project image is normally
  build-varambuf/zephyr/zephyr.elf, built from src/main.c. Do not substitute the
  separate HIL zephyr-workload image or Renode app. Resolve source locations
  and symbol addresses from the loaded matching ELF, never this file's history
  or a cached watch address. Stop on an image/source mismatch and explain it.
- Keep existing hardware ownership. Before a new hardware attach, follow the
  workspace's exact-probe ownership rules; do not steal a claim or start a
  second debug session. Release any leases/lock-holder processes this task
  acquired at every handoff; never release another task's lease. If continued
  use needs an ownership transfer, arrange it explicitly with Eddie.
- No build, flash, reset, erase, power cycle, firmware fix, memory/expression
  write, disconnect or automatic reconnect is part of this rehearsal. Temporary
  breakpoints and negotiated observation streams are allowed. Preserve Eddie's
  existing breakpoints and captures. Stop/Take control always takes precedence;
  a revoked grant is not permission to reacquire it silently.

Filming setup (one brief reminder, not a repeated checklist)
- Ask Eddie to enable Follow in the debug toolbar so source/frame navigation
  follows real stops and selections. Feedback highlights affected panels and
  is optional. Data and Evidence update even with Follow off. Do not claim the
  MCP tool can toggle these, move panes or change a graph's style.
- Before connecting, REC -> Evidence -> Recording settings: enable Record
  debug sessions, choose Full core stack frames and RTOS task inspection if
  wanted, and Save recording options. More detail adds halt inspection time.
  These settings apply to the next connection. Never end an existing session
  just to change them. If this session is unrecorded, state that markers/replay
  will be unavailable and continue the live tour unless Eddie requests a restart.
- In RTOS, use Auto/Zephyr, Auto refresh on halt, Auto scan selected stack,
  and Live task activity with source Samples. Keep RTOS visible while sampling;
  this UI sampling mode pauses when hidden. These are UI controls, not a
  side effect of rtos.tasks. If setup is missing, ask once and yield for it;
  do not repeat a confirmation Eddie already gave.
- For a visible live graph, use the Symbols watch for sineVal with Graph
  enabled; heartbeatCount and tick_counter are useful additional values.
  Check that each watch resolves to the current ELF, especially after a rebuild.
  Do not use a saved numerical address as proof of the symbol's location.
  Preserve an existing stream; only start/extend a watch through the negotiated
  schema if it supports doing so without replacing Eddie's watches. Otherwise
  let Eddie configure the graph and use expression reads for the tour.

Pacing and evidence
- One short lead-in per group, then consecutive sequential tool calls as soon
  as results arrive. Check success, freshness and control each time. No repeated
  catalog/describe/status calls when the existing result is sufficient, no
  paragraphs between routine calls, and no permission questions for work already
  authorized within the act. Summarize actual findings in one or two sentences.
- Every source step is a separate run.step with count:1 and the negotiated
  source-step mode. Never batch the three steps. No presentation sleeps between
  successful steps. Optimized code may skip/repeat source lines or lose locals;
  say so rather than promising a particular line sequence.
- Continue/run-to uses wait:false. Use bounded waits (at most five seconds per
  attempt) and inspect the actual stop. A wait timeout does not prove a halt.
  Pause a still-running core before inspection. A step timeout may also leave
  it running: refresh status, pause with the valid grant, and do not blindly
  repeat the step. No unbounded retries or stepping through the entire kernel.
- Keep the three-second warm-up and two-second sampling window below. Prefer
  a bounded debugger wait; a terminal sleep is allowed only for those running
  acquisition windows. It is not allowed to issue debugger commands.
- Use recording.mark for the specified checkpoints when recording is active.
  Include real observed values and a relevant source location using its schema.
  Only say a marker was saved after a successful receipt; otherwise state the
  limitation once. Evidence refreshes on markers; do not pretend to click it.
- Distinguish halt snapshots, sampled task activity, sampled memory graphs and
  recorder events. Neither sampling nor session recording proves lossless RTOS
  tracing. This build uses the ViewAlyzer RAM recorder; that does not by itself
  connect a capture to the IDE. Do not start recorder drain, SWO, ETM or DWT
  acquisition as a surprise addition, or claim unavailable instruction history.

Act 1: live RTOS tour and visible stepping
1. Read status and the project's IDE configuration, obtain control, then reuse
   the target session or attach after approval using the decision above. Do not
   require a live target identity before attach; use the identity it returns.
   Once attached, verify the actual target and loaded source/image identity.
   Confirm the source and symbols below before installing any breakpoint.
2. Warm up: resume for about three seconds, then pause. Remove only your own
   temporary startup breakpoint if it prevents execution. Discover Zephyr with
   rtos.detect and read rtos.tasks with stack_usage:true. Show task identities,
   states, priorities and available stack usage. Use actual returned names;
   cont_low_tid, cont_med_tid and cont_high_tid are source identifiers, not
   guaranteed display names. DynNamed and nap_tid are additional candidates.
3. Resume for two seconds so activity with known task names and live symbol
   samples accumulates, then pause. Inspect a currently blocked/suspended task
   through rtos.task.open, stack.list and available locals, keeping its context
   explicit. Prefer a contention task or nap task if present. Do not call the
   saved task stack the currently executing core's stack. Return to live core.
   Mark "Zephyr task tour" with the real task/stack observations.
4. Resolve a source breakpoint inside the recurring contention_low_task loop
   at lowPrioAccess++ in src/main.c. Do not break at the function entry: this
   thread may already have entered its infinite loop. Resume to the resolved
   location and verify the actual function/line. Announce the three steps once,
   then make three individual source-step-over calls, each count:1. These cover
   application statements rather than intentionally entering a blocking kernel
   call. Check each result and summarize the actual line progression. Remove
   only the breakpoint you created. Mark "Three source steps" with those lines.
5. Read sineVal, heartbeatCount, tick_counter and sharedCounter in a bounded
   group if supported (otherwise consecutive reads). Resume for two seconds,
   then pause and read them again. Show only the changes actually observed;
   sineVal is periodic, so an equal pair alone does not establish a stall.
   Mark "Live values observed" with useful before/after values.
6. Ensure the core is paused and your temporary breakpoint is removed. Release
   control and verify handoff. Say: "Your turn: inspect a task, select a frame
   or single-step. Tell me Continue Act 2 for the contention investigation."
   End the turn with the session and recording still connected.

Act 2: catch the high-priority task after its mutex wait
1. On Eddie's request, get fresh status and a fresh approved control grant.
   Inspect any changed stop. Read the contention_high_task loop, then resolve
   a source breakpoint at highPrioAccess++ after waitTime is calculated. This
   stops on a recurring successful lock acquisition, not the one-time entry.
2. Resume to that breakpoint with a bounded wait. Verify the actual stop and
   inspect the live call stack, available start/waitTime locals, and
   highPrioAccess, medPrioAccess, lowPrioAccess and contentionCounter. If locals
   are optimized out, say so; use the counters/source without inventing a wait
   duration. A zero wait is a valid observation; allow at most one further hit
   if useful, then continue with whatever was actually observed.
3. Mark "High-priority mutex acquisition" with the measured wait if available
   and the actual stack/counters. Do not present debugger-disturbed timing as
   an undisturbed benchmark. Save the causal explanation for Act 3.
4. Remove your breakpoint, keep the core paused, release and verify handoff.
   Say: "Your turn: inspect the stack and locals. Tell me Continue Act 3 to
   investigate who holds the mutex and why the higher-priority task can wait."
   End the turn; do not resume, end or reconnect the session.

Act 3: explain the contention from observed state and source
1. Obtain fresh status/control. Resolve a source breakpoint on the
   k_mutex_unlock(&contention_test_mutex) line in contention_low_task, after
   its k_msleep(20). Resume to it with a bounded wait and verify the location.
2. Read a fresh task inventory and inspect contention_test_mutex using typed
   expression/child reads. Use DWARF-reported fields; do not invent a kernel
   struct layout. Correlate any reported owner address with the task inventory.
   Inspect a blocked contention task's saved stack/locals if one is present,
   then return to the live core. Do not claim a blocked waiter was captured
   merely because the source can create one. State unavailable evidence.
3. Compare contention_low_task's lock -> work -> sleep(20 ms) -> unlock with
   normal_low_task's short critical section. Explain the source-supported
   mechanism and which parts the live snapshot corroborates. Report priority
   inheritance only if actual owner/priority evidence supports it. Distinguish
   this intentional demonstration from a newly discovered product defect.
4. Mark "Contention investigation" with the observed owner, task state, source
   location and conclusions that the evidence supports. Remove your temporary
   breakpoint, ensure the core is paused, release control and verify completion.
   Leave the firmware unchanged and the session/recording connected for Eddie.
   Say: "Your turn. The saved markers are in Evidence. When you choose to End
   the session, open its recording in Evidence to inspect the captured stops."
   Do not end the session or open replay automatically. Offline replay requires
   disconnection and no pending target operation; it only contains captured data.

If Eddie takes control or any required operation fails, stop the affected
sequence, report the actual state and yield. Do not turn a filming rehearsal
into open-ended tool debugging. Preserve the session and existing evidence.
